\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Conceptual comparison between conventional survey-based mobility modeling and the proposed Projection--Inference Gravity Framework (PIGF). Existing approaches estimate behavioral mechanisms from observed origin--destination flows or individual trajectories before predicting mobility patterns. In contrast, PIGF reformulates survey-free mobility prediction as a behavioral inference problem by recovering city-specific distance-decay functions directly from aggregate trip-length distributions and integrating them with transferable production estimation and analytical destination attraction.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:conceptual_framework}{{1}{4}{Conceptual comparison between conventional survey-based mobility modeling and the proposed Projection--Inference Gravity Framework (PIGF). Existing approaches estimate behavioral mechanisms from observed origin--destination flows or individual trajectories before predicting mobility patterns. In contrast, PIGF reformulates survey-free mobility prediction as a behavioral inference problem by recovering city-specific distance-decay functions directly from aggregate trip-length distributions and integrating them with transferable production estimation and analytical destination attraction}{figure.1}{}}
